<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<templates xml:space="preserve">

    <!-- A Refund button in the top bar, beside Register and Orders.

         Returns happen often enough here to deserve a button of their own, and
         the route to one had a trap in the middle: the orders list opens on
         Active orders, a finished sale is not among them, so the sale being
         returned appears to be missing until someone thinks to change the
         filter to Paid. This lands on that filter directly.

         Added rather than replacing Register. Register is the only one-tap way
         back to the product grid, and without it a cashier who opens Orders
         has no obvious route back to ringing up a sale.

         Shown on a permission, because a refund takes money back out of the
         drawer and a shop may well want to name who can give one. The
         capability ships attached to "Use Point of Sale (Till)", so nothing
         changes for a shop that has not thought about it: every cashier keeps
         the button, exactly as before. Moving it onto the narrower "Give
         Refunds at the Till" permission is how a shop restricts it. -->
    <t t-inherit="point_of_sale.Navbar" t-inherit-mode="extension">
        <xpath expr="//button[hasclass('orders-button')]" position="after">
            <button t-if="pos.getCashier()?._can_refund"
                    class="pos-retail-refund-btn btn btn-lg btn-light lh-lg"
                    t-on-click="() => this.posRetailStartRefund()">
                <span t-if="!ui.isSmall"><i class="fa fa-undo me-1"/>Refund</span>
                <i t-else="" class="fa fa-fw fa-undo fa-lg"/>
            </button>
        </xpath>
    </t>

</templates>
